  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Fixed the Default PersianCalendarWithClock template ignoring its Background property. The outer border now uses TemplateBinding, with the previous default color retained in the style setter.\n\nFixed background customization of the calendar and clock embedded in PersianCalendarWithClock, including usage inside PersianDateTimePicker.\n\nTransparent backgrounds are now supplied by theme-specific child styles instead of local property values, allowing custom CalendarStyle and ClockStyle background setters to take effect.\n\nStandalone calendar and clock styles remain unchanged. Default render comparisons were identical in six themes; LightClassic showed a small calendar/header alignment difference.\n\nFixed\n- Custom CalendarStyle and ClockStyle setters can now change the Background and BorderThickness of the calendar and clock embedded in PersianCalendarWithClock, including usage inside PersianDateTimePicker.\n- Moved local appearance values into theme-specific child styles, retaining transparent backgrounds and zero borders as defaults.\n\nValidation\n- Custom background and border setters verified across all seven themes.\n- All supported .NET targets build successfully.\n\nKnown visual difference\n- A small calendar/header alignment difference remains in LightClassic compared with the previous implementation.\n\nStandalone calendar and clock styles remain unchanged.\n\n\n\nCalendar events can now customize their day highlight. Set `CalendarEvent.HighlightStyle` for local events or pass `highlightStyle` when creating a `CalendarEventDefinition` for a provider.\n\nWhen multiple events share a day, the calendar prefers a local event style over a provider event style. Within each source, the highest-priority event with a style wins. If no event supplies a style, the calendar uses the control�s `CalendarEventHighlightStyle`, then the active theme�s default. The event list keeps its existing sort order.",
